Report volume changes made on the device back to the server.

HookVolumeController only ever pushed the server's volume out through the
hook; its start_monitoring was a no-op, so a level changed on the Echo itself
(the buttons, Home Assistant) never reached Music Assistant, which kept a
stale slider - and so did the Spotify app through MA's "Sync Spotify app
volume", which then pushed the stale level back on the next change.

--hook-volume-state names a file holding the device's own level 0-100
(biscuit-audio's /run/biscuit-audio/volume, replaced atomically). It is
polled, and a level that is not explained by a hook request is reported
through the volume callback, unmuted. Two things keep the hook and the
watcher from driving each other:

  * for 2 s after a hook request the file is not judged at all: it still
    holds the previous level for a moment, and during a slider drag it
    trails the latest request by several values. A device change made in
    that window is reported when it ends, not lost;
  * a level within 2 points of the request is the device's quantisation of
    it (30 steps, 3.3 points apart), not a change. Once the file settles
    there, that actual step is what later levels are compared against, so
    a single button step is never swallowed by the tolerance.

get_state also prefers the file to the persisted level, so a change made
while sendspin was not running is picked up at start.

